  <static seed="623" source="Library/WHFreeman/Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition/14_Differentiation_in_Several_Variables/14.4_Differentiability_and_Tangent_Planes/14.4.7.pg">
    <statement><p>Find an equation of the tangent plane at the given point:</p>
<p><m>F(r,s)=2r^{4}\frac{1}{s^{0.5}}+\frac{1}{s^{3}}, \qquad (2,1)</m></p>
<p><m>z=</m><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>64r-19s-76</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(2,1)</m> is 
<me>z=f(2,1)+f_r(2,1)(r-2)+f_s(2,1)(s-1)</me>

We compute the value of the function and its partial derivatives at the point <m>(2,1)</m>:
<me>\left. \begin{array}{lcl}
f(r,s)=2r^{4}\frac{1}{s^{0.5}}+\frac{1}{s^{3}} \amp   \amp  f(2,1)=33 \\
f_r(r,s)=8r^{3}\frac{1}{s^{0.5}} \amp  \Rightarrow \amp  f_r(2,1)=64 \\
f_s(r,s)=-\left(r^{4}\frac{1}{s^{1.5}}+3\frac{1}{s^{4}}\right) \amp   \amp  f_s(2,1)=-19 \end{array} \right.</me>

We substitute these values to obtain the following equation of the tangent plane:
<me>z=33+64\mathopen{}\left(r-2\right)-19\mathopen{}\left(s-1\right)=64r-19s-76</me>





</solution>
